        content: `REFUSING SERVICE, CONFLICT DE-ESCALATION & INCIDENT DOCUMENTATION

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 1: THE LEGAL RIGHT AND RESPONSIBILITY TO REFUSE SERVICE
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Refusing service to a patron is one of the most critical and difficult skills required of RSA-certified staff. It is also one that many new workers fear — the worry of causing a confrontation, upsetting a regular, or making a mistake causes many staff to hesitate or avoid refusal when it is clearly warranted. This hesitation has serious legal and safety consequences.

The important realities to internalise:
→ Refusing service to an intoxicated person is not optional — it is a legal requirement. Under Australian liquor licensing legislation, you have both the legal right and the statutory duty to refuse alcohol service to any person who is visibly intoxicated, underage, or disorderly.
→ The responsibility lies with you as the server, not with your manager or with the patron. If a manager pressures you to continue serving an intoxicated patron, this does not remove your personal legal liability — you can still be personally prosecuted.
→ A refusal is not a personal rejection of the patron — it is an enforcement of the law. Framing it this way in your mind — and in your words to the patron — is the foundation of a professional, effective refusal.

WHEN EXACTLY MUST YOU REFUSE?
You must refuse service when:
→ A patron is visibly intoxicated (showing the signs described in Module 2).
→ You cannot verify that a patron is 18 years of age or older.
→ A patron is being disorderly, aggressive, or threatening — even if they are not intoxicated.
→ A patron attempts secondary supply — purchasing alcohol for a person who has been refused.
→ The service would breach a condition of the venue's liquor licence — for example, a mine site wet mess with a daily drink limit (commonly 4 standard drinks per shift per worker) where a patron has already reached their limit.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 2: THE PROFESSIONAL REFUSAL TECHNIQUE — STEP BY STEP
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The goal of a professional refusal is to communicate the decision clearly and firmly, without unnecessary confrontation, preserving the dignity of the patron wherever possible, and protecting the safety of everyone in the venue. The following five-step technique is grounded in licensed venue practice across Australia.

STEP 1: EARLY INTERVENTION — ACT BEFORE THE CRISIS
The best refusal is the one that prevents a patron from reaching an advanced level of intoxication in the first place. When you begin noticing the early signs — the slight slurring, the increased volume, the loss of coordination — begin slowing service naturally. Offer water and food. Reduce the speed of re-service. This is early intervention, and it is the professional's preferred approach.

If you allow a patron to reach severe intoxication before acting, the refusal conversation is far more difficult, potentially confrontational, and legally more complex (because they are now more cognitively impaired and therefore less capable of understanding and accepting the refusal calmly).

STEP 2: SPEAK PRIVATELY AND RESPECTFULLY
When you must refuse service, approach the patron privately — away from their peers and away from other patrons if at all possible. Being told in front of friends that you are cut off is publicly humiliating and dramatically increases the likelihood of an angry, defensive reaction.

Choose a moment when the patron is not mid-conversation with others. Speak calmly, at a normal conversational volume (not loudly, which draws attention and escalates tension).

STEP 3: STATE THE LAW AND VENUE POLICY — NOT YOUR PERSONAL JUDGMENT
This is critical: the refusal must be framed as a legal and policy requirement, not as your personal opinion about the patron's condition. This removes the confrontation from being "you versus the patron" and reframes it as "the law requires this."

Effective phrasings:
→ "Under state liquor legislation, I have a legal obligation not to serve you any more alcohol tonight. I'd be personally breaking the law if I did, and I could lose my job. I'm not able to do that."
→ "Our venue has a strictly enforced responsible service policy, and I need to let you know that I can't serve you any more alcohol this evening."
→ "I've noticed you're showing some signs of having had enough for tonight — things like [specific observable behaviour]. I'm not able to continue service. I'm sorry about that."

Notice that the third example references a specific observable behaviour rather than making a blanket accusation. This is important because accusatory language ("you're drunk", "you're a mess") is provocative. Observable behaviour ("I noticed your speech is a bit slurred" or "I noticed you had a bit of difficulty with your footing just now") is factual and less personally offensive.

STEP 4: REMAIN CALM, FIRM, AND NON-NEGOTIABLE
The patron will very commonly respond with one or more of the following:
→ Denial: "I've only had two drinks." (Regardless of the actual count, refusal must stand if visible signs are present.)
→ Bargaining: "Just one more." (The answer is no. Always. Once a refusal decision is made, it cannot be reversed.)
→ Aggression: Raised voice, swearing, threats.
→ Social pressure: "My mates are all still drinking." (Irrelevant to the refusal decision for this individual.)

Your responses:
→ Maintain a calm, steady, quiet tone. Do not raise your voice to match theirs.
→ Keep your body language open and non-threatening: hands open, relaxed posture, no finger-pointing, maintain comfortable distance.
→ Repeat the key message simply and calmly without elaborating or debating: "I understand you're frustrated. I'm not able to serve you any more alcohol tonight. I'm happy to get you a water or something to eat."
→ Do not argue, debate, or try to convince the patron that they are intoxicated — this never works and escalates conflict.
→ Do not make it personal: avoid "I think you've had enough" — it invites a debate about your assessment. Stick to "I'm not able to serve you."

STEP 5: OFFER ALTERNATIVES AND ASSIST WITH SAFE EGRESS
A professional refusal does not simply end with "no." It offers constructive alternatives:
→ Offer non-alcoholic beverages — water, juice, soft drinks, coffee, hot food.
→ If the patron needs to leave the venue, actively assist them in arranging safe transport. On a mine site, this means contacting the wet mess supervisor, camp coordinator, or security to escort the worker safely back to their accommodation. Do not allow a visibly intoxicated person to attempt to drive a vehicle or operate any equipment.
→ If possible, hand off the patron to a sober friend who can ensure their safety.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 3: CONFLICT DE-ESCALATION WITH AGGRESSIVE PATRONS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Despite best efforts, some refusals will result in aggressive responses. Alcohol-induced disinhibition, pride, frustration, and peer pressure combine to make some patrons react angrily. The following de-escalation techniques are essential:

PHYSICAL POSITIONING AND BODY LANGUAGE
→ Maintain an open, non-threatening posture. Keep both hands relaxed and visible in front of you — do not cross your arms (signals defensiveness) or point fingers (signals aggression).
→ Maintain an appropriate safe distance — at least an arm's length from the patron. This gives you reaction time and does not feel invasive to the patron.
→ NEVER touch an intoxicated or aggressive patron unless you are being physically threatened and restraint is the only option to prevent imminent injury — and if so, use the minimum force necessary and call security immediately.
→ Position yourself with your back to the bar or a wall, with a clear escape route available to you. Never allow yourself to be cornered.

VOCAL DE-ESCALATION TECHNIQUES
→ Use the person's name if you know it — this humanises the interaction.
→ Speak at a lower volume than normal. This is counterintuitive but highly effective — when you lower your voice, it forces the other person to stop shouting in order to hear you, and this itself reduces aggression.
→ Acknowledge their feelings without agreeing with their demand: "I can see you're annoyed, and I understand that. I still can't serve you more alcohol tonight."
→ Avoid ultimatums and "if you don't..." statements — these escalate rather than de-escalate.

WHEN TO CALL FOR BACKUP
If the patron:
→ Refuses to accept the refusal and continues demanding service after your second or third calm repetition.
→ Raises their voice to the point of disturbing other patrons.
→ Uses threatening language toward staff or other patrons.
→ Becomes physically aggressive.

You must immediately contact your supervisor or security. In a mine site wet mess, this typically means contacting site security or the camp manager. In a licensed venue, this means signalling venue security.

WHEN TO CALL POLICE
You must call police (Triple Zero — 000) if:
→ The patron physically assaults a staff member or another patron.
→ The patron refuses to leave the licensed area after being given a reasonable time and reasonable opportunity to do so (remaining on licensed premises while intoxicated and having been refused service is a criminal offence in most jurisdictions).
→ The patron is in possession of a weapon.
→ Any person's safety is at immediate risk.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 4: INCIDENT REGISTER — DOCUMENTATION AND LEGAL PROTECTION
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Every refusal of service, physical removal, aggressive incident, or police call at a licensed venue must be formally documented in the venue's Incident Register. This is not optional — it is a legal requirement under liquor licensing regulations across Australia.

Why documentation matters:
→ It creates a contemporaneous (written at the time of the event) record that can be used as evidence in your defence if you are later investigated or prosecuted.
→ It protects the licensee by demonstrating that the venue is proactively managing harm.
→ It identifies patterns — a patron who creates an incident every Friday night, or particular events that consistently result in problems.
→ It forms part of the venue's compliance record reviewed by liquor licensing inspectors.

What must be recorded:
→ Date and exact time of the incident.
→ Detailed description of the patron's appearance (height, build, clothing, hair colour) — not their name, which you may not know.
→ Specific visible signs of intoxication or disorderly behaviour observed — written in factual, objective language.
→ The exact action taken (refused service, asked to leave, called security, called police).
→ The names of all staff members involved or who witnessed the incident.
→ The outcome — did the patron leave voluntarily, were they escorted out, was police notified, were police called, was first aid required?
→ If police attended — the officers' names and badge numbers, and the police event number.

Record entries must be made in ink (not pencil), with no blank spaces, and must not be altered or erased after the fact. Any correction must be made with a single line through the error, initialled and dated. Falsification or destruction of incident register records is a criminal offence.`
